pub const LESSON_GOALS : [27]str = [
    "Vibecoding is a loop of three steps: you describe what you want, a program runs, and a check says yes or no.",
    "A program is a list of small steps done in order, and the order is part of what the program means.",
    "A sentence that sounds right can be settled by a small case and a test, so you can check a myth instead of arguing about it.",
    "How to work with an agent in your editor: you describe the change, it edits, and you decide what stays.",
    "Ten terminal commands that cover a first day, and the one of them that cannot be undone.",
    "How to read an error message part by part, and four shortcuts that save time.",
    "What a commit keeps, why editing afterwards cannot change it, and when to make one.",
    "How a try on a branch is thrown away or merged, and why main is untouched until you choose.",
    "Why a key comes in two halves, which half you may share, and which half never leaves your machine.",
    "What the four parts of a prompt are, and how a missing part shows up as a missing bit.",
    "Why one example and its wanted output pins down a rule better than an adjective, and why a second example is worth adding.",
    "Why a plan of small steps with a check each leaves one suspect when something fails.",
    "Why work goes through four phases in order, and why a failed test sends it back to build and never forward to ship.",
    "How a round of ask, run, read and fix removes one problem, and why a round that skips the reading hides the rest.",
    "How to count the same error coming back, and how that count picks your next move: read it, ask smaller, go back to the save point, start fresh.",
    "How a test compares what the code gave with the answer you wrote down first, and why a yes means only what the test asked.",
    "Why a test that has never failed proves little, and how one planted sign shows which test can see a bug.",
    "How to read an agent's diff against your request and spot a change nobody asked for.",
    "Why a program asks an agent for an answer with a fixed shape, and how it refuses a reply whose shape is wrong.",
    "What a tool is, which tools an agent may start at once, and which one waits for your yes.",
    "What an agent still sees of a long chat, what falls out of its window, and what a file in the project keeps.",
    "How a bot waits for a message, picks a reply from a table and goes back to waiting, and why the same message always gets the same reply.",
    "Why a bot starts as a table of commands and replies, and how three checks catch a bad table before any bot exists.",
    "Why the name of a secret may live in the repository while its value may not, whether in a file or in a prompt.",
    "How to say an idea as one input, one output and one test, and why an idea with no test is not ready to hand to an agent.",
    "Why one number read from a run beats a feeling, and what one run cannot tell you.",
    "How to write a plan whose every line is either measured or plainly not known yet, with no promise about the future."
];
pub const LESSON_TEXTS : [27]str = [
    "Vibecoding means you describe what you want in plain words and an AI agent writes the program. Your part does not end there. You run it, and you check the result against your description, because a program can finish without an error and still be wrong. In the spec of this lesson a cafe tab is the number of drinks times the price of one drink: 3 drinks at 4 each is 12. A run that adds instead of multiplying prints 7, finishes cleanly, and is rejected by the check. The check is a test, and the test judges the result, not the person or the program that wrote it.",
    "A program is a list of small steps that the computer does one after another. Take the number 2. Add 3, then double it: you get 10. Double it, then add 3: you get 7. The same two steps in another order give another answer, so when you describe a program to an agent you describe the order too. A loop is one step written once and repeated: add 3 four times, from 2, gives 14. When a result surprises you, read the steps in order and find the first one that does not do what you meant.",
    "Five sentences about vibecoding sound right and are not. If it runs without an error, it is right: the average of 4 and 8 written as a plus b over 2 prints 8, and the answer is 6. One passing test proves the code is right: x times x equals x plus x at 2, and not at 3. The agent remembers the whole conversation: it reads a window, 8 messages of the 20 sent in this case. A step can always be undone: 7 halved and doubled is 6. A check that passes is a good check: one that says yes to everything also says yes to 2 against 3. Each myth is a small case, and a test settles it.",
    "An editor is where you read and change files. An agent sits beside it: you describe a change in words and it edits the files for you. It edits fast, but you are the one who keeps or rejects each change. The rule in the spec is two checks: can you read the whole change, and do the tests still pass? A change of 3 lines that passes is kept. A change of 12 lines that fails is sent back. A rewrite of 90 lines is sent back even when it passes, because nobody has read it. The spec sets the reading limit at 20 lines; pick your own and keep to it.",
    "The terminal is a window where you type a command and press Enter. Ten commands go a long way: pwd, ls, cd, mkdir, touch, cat, cp, mv, rm and clear. They are safe to try, with one exception: rm deletes a file with no undo, so read it twice before you press Enter. The spec lists all 10 with a one-line meaning each and marks exactly 1 of them as having no undo.",
    "An error message looks like noise, but it has 4 parts in the same order every time: the kind of error, what it says, the file and the line. The line is where the problem was noticed, so the cause can sit just above it: start reading 1 line before. Keep 4 shortcuts at hand: Cmd+S saves, Cmd+Z undoes, Ctrl+C stops a program in the terminal, and the up arrow brings back the previous command. When you ask your agent for help, paste the whole message, not a retelling of it.",
    "Vibecoding means an agent changes your files while you watch, and some changes break things. A commit is a save point: it keeps every file exactly as it was at that moment, and you can edit as much as you like afterwards, the saved copy does not change. In commit.t27 the file said 10 when it was committed; an agent then made it 99; going back returns 10, not 99. Each commit adds one save point to the history and overwrites nothing: after a third commit there are 3. Commit when the program runs, before you ask for the next change.",
    "A branch is a copy of your line of work where you can try something, and the main line stays as it was until you decide. In branch.t27 main holds 10 and an agent's try turns it into 99 on its own branch. If the try is bad you delete the branch and main still holds 10. If it is good you merge it and main holds 99. While you try there are 2 lines of work, main and the try; after either ending there is 1. Ask an agent for a risky change on a branch, not on main.",
    "To prove who you are to a code host without a password you make a key pair: a public half you hand out and a private half that never leaves your machine. The host keeps the public half and sends a challenge that only the private half can answer. In key_pair.t27 a pair has 2 halves, 1 is shared and 1 is kept, and 0 copies of the private half are ever sent to a host, a chat or a repository. The key text in the spec is a made-up placeholder, not a key. Never paste a real private half into a prompt: an agent that sees it has it.",
    "A prompt that works has four parts: a goal, the context the agent cannot see, the limits it must keep, and the check that tells you it is done. In prompt_parts.t27 each part is one bit of a number, so a prompt becomes a mask: the vague prompt Make it better is mask 0, a goal alone is mask 1, and a full prompt with all four parts is mask 15. The tests read the bits straight from the constants. The lesson plants one bug: give the check the same bit as the limit, and the four parts no longer add up to 15, so a test fails.",
    "An adjective such as bigger or shorter fits many rules, so the agent picks one of them, and not always yours. An example input with the output you want fits far fewer. In prompt_example.t27 the rule you mean is add 3, and the examples are 1 to 4, 2 to 5 and 5 to 8. The first example fits two rules, add 3 and multiply by 3 then add 1, so it cannot choose between them. The second example, 2 to 5, rules the other one out, because it would give 7. The lesson plants one bug: change the step from 3 to 2, and the examples fail.",
    "A big request is a chain of small ones, and the agent can get any link wrong. In small_step.t27 a plan has five steps, and each ends with a check you run before the next prompt. The list PASSED holds 1 for a step whose check passed and 0 for one that did not: 1, 1, 0, 0, 0. The first zero is step 3, so step 3 is the only prompt to fix. One big prompt for all five steps leaves five suspects; one small step leaves one. The lesson plants one bug: say that step 4 was the first to fail, and the test that reads the list catches it.",
    "Every piece of work with an agent has the same four phases. Idea: you say in one sentence what you want. Build: the agent writes one small piece. Test: you run it and a test says yes or no. Ship: you save it and put it where it is used. The phases are not a straight line. When the test says no, the work goes back to build, not forward to ship, and the loop runs again. The spec of this lesson holds two tables: where each phase goes next when the tests pass, and where it goes when they fail. The player runs its 4 tests. Change one entry of the fail table so that a failed test goes on to ship, and exactly one test goes red.",
    "A round has 4 steps. Ask the agent for one change. Run the program. Read what came back, all of it. Fix one problem and go round again. Each round removes one problem, so a program with 3 problems is green after 3 rounds. The spec keeps a table of the problems left after each round: 3, 2, 1, 0. The shortcut that breaks the loop is a round that says done without reading the result. In the widget you can switch the reading off and watch the counter lie. In the spec the same shortcut is one changed number, and exactly one test goes red.",
    "Getting stuck is normal; going round in a circle is the signal to change what you do. Count how many times in a row the same error came back. The first time, read the error and say in your own words what it claims. The second time, ask for a smaller step. The third time, go back to the last save point, the commit that worked. After that, start a fresh conversation with a short summary and the one error. A different error resets the count, because it means you got past the last one. The spec holds this as a table of moves indexed by the count, and its 4 tests check each rung.",
    "You ask an agent for a function that gives the larger of two numbers. Do not read its code yet: first write down the answer you expect for 3 and 9, which is 9. A test runs the code and the judge compares: yes if it gave 9, no if it gave anything else. The judge sees only the answer, so a yes means the code agrees with you for those inputs and nothing more. A second version of the function with a slip in it, always giving the first number, gives 3, and the judge says no.",
    "A test that has never failed may be looking in the wrong place. Take the rule that an adult is 18 or older and plant a bug: change the sign so that 18 itself no longer counts. A weak test on ages 30 and 5 stays green with the bug in, because neither age is near the edge. A test on age 18 turns red. Bugs live at edges, so put a test there, and plant a bug to see it fail.",
    "An agent says done and shows a diff: the lines it removed and the lines it added. Read it against what you asked for. If you asked for a change to lines 10 to 20 and the diff also touches line 40, that is a change nobody requested, and now you have to read it. Accept a diff whose every touched line is in the request. Send back one that is not, or ask why. Even a clean diff still goes through the tests.",
    "When you chat with an agent you read free text. When a program reads the answer, free text is a problem: it cannot tell a sentence from a request to run something. So the agent is asked for a reply with a shape: a kind, and the arguments that kind needs. This spec has 3 kinds: 0 say, 1 ask and 2 call a tool. A sentence needs no argument; a question and a tool call need at least 1. The program checks the shape first and acts only if it fits. A tool call that names no file is refused before anything runs. The negative control plants the bug in one place: set NEEDS_CALL to 0 and the test the_bad_reply_is_refused turns red, because a reply with no arguments is no longer too short.",
    "An agent only reads and writes text. It cannot open a file or run a test by itself. A tool is a function the host runs for it: the agent names the tool, the host checks the name against a list, runs it and sends the result back as text. This spec lists 3 tools: 0 read a file, 1 run the tests and 2 write a file. Reading and testing change nothing, so they run at once; writing changes your project, so it waits for your yes. The loop is capped too: of 9 calls asked in one turn the host lets 5 through, so a stuck agent cannot call tools forever. Plant the bug by setting FREE_BELOW to 3 and the test writing_waits_for_a_yes turns red: writing would run without asking.",
    "An agent does not remember your project; it sees the last messages of the chat, as many as fit in its window. The spec uses a window of 6 and a chat of 10, small enough to trace by hand. The agent keeps 6 messages and forgets 4: messages 0 to 3 have fallen out, and the oldest one it still sees is number 4. What you write into a file of the project stays, because the agent can read the file again in a new chat. That is why a rule you want kept goes into a file, not into one more message. Plant the bug by setting WINDOW to 7 and two tests turn red, because the window no longer ends where the chat does.",
    "A bot is not magic. It is a loop with four beats: wait for a message, read it, pick a reply, send it, and wait again. In message_loop.t27 a message is a small number: 1 is start, 2 is help, 3 is stop, and anything else is not understood. A table maps each message to a reply, so one turn of the loop is one lookup. The inbox holds 5 messages and the loop stops at the stop message, so it answers 4. Send the widget a few messages and notice that the same message always gets the same reply.",
    "You do not write a bot, you describe it. spec_to_bot.t27 lists 3 commands and the reply each one gets, as data. Before anything is generated, 3 checks run on that description: every command has a reply, no two commands share a reply, and the number of routes equals the number of commands. A description that fails a check would make a wrong bot, so the table is fixed first and the bot is generated afterwards. Blank a reply in the widget and see which check turns red.",
    "A secret is a value that opens something, such as the token of a bot. The code names it, BOT_TOKEN, and never holds it. The value lives in a file called .env on your machine, and git is told to ignore that file. The repository keeps only .env.example, with a made-up placeholder. In secrets_out.t27 a file that holds a secret is never a file git saves, and the same goes for a message to an agent: write the name of the secret, not its value. A secret that was ever saved or pasted must be treated as lost and replaced.",
    "An agent can build only what you can say. The smallest thing you can say about an idea has three parts: one input, one output and one test that tells you it is done. Take a made-up reading log: the input is one book title, the output is how many books are logged, and the test is that after one title the count is 1. The spec of this lesson holds those three as numbers and checks them with 4 tests. Ready means one of each: 1 x 1 x 1 = 1. Set the test count to 0 and the tests that name it go red, because an idea with no test is not ready to hand over. Your own idea can be as small as this one.",
    "When your project runs, pick one thing to measure and read it from the run itself. Made-up example: the reading log was run on 4 titles and kept 3. The share kept is 3 x 100 / 4 = 75 out of 100. That is the whole fact: one run, one number. It does not say what the next run will give, so the widget lists that as nothing yet. The spec has 3 tests: a measure needs at least one run, a run cannot keep more than it was given, and the share is read from the run. Set KEPT to 5 and two of them go red.",
    "A plan for the next month is honest when each line is one of two kinds: a thing you measured, with the run that printed it, or a thing you do not know yet, with the run that would tell you. A line that promises a result nobody has measured does not belong in it. The made-up plan for the reading log has 5 lines: 2 measured, 3 unknown and 0 promised, and 2 + 3 + 0 = 5. The spec has 4 tests: the lines add up, something is measured, something is unknown and nothing is promised. Set PROMISED to 1 and two of them go red. This lesson gives no forecast of any kind: it is a way of writing, not a prediction."
];
